From d7d996e7fe49fd5a42246ec97f6a45da9edf65f4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jon Bradley <jon.bradley@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 07:58:18 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] Map positional record initializers onto members, not element
 slots

A brace initializer's elements were always numbered as array elements, so a
record initializer wrote element paths while every later read resolved to
member paths. The two never met and the taint was dropped:

    struct P { int x; int y; };
    struct P p = { v, 0 };   // wrote p.deref
    return p.x;              // read  p.x     -> summary empty

This is a silent soundness false negative, not an over-approximation: a write
at one path is not observed at a read of another (propagation goes through
substitute_prefix), so nothing downstream recovers it. It affected the flat
case above, a record nested in a record, and an array of records. Designated
initializers were already correct -- they name their destination outright.

Add a per-translation-unit record layout registry (Context::struct_layouts,
tag -> members in declaration order) and use it to place a record's positional
elements on the members those positions name. Each member also carries the tag
of its own record type, so a brace nested at a member's position recurses with
that member's layout; an array carries its element type's layout down to the
level where the elements actually are (tracked by declarator rank, so
`int m[2][2]` is unaffected and `struct Q qs[2][2]` still resolves).

Deliberately excluded: pointer and array members never carry a type tag. They
are not stored inline, so recursing into a brace at their position with the
pointee's layout would write a wrong path rather than fix one. This also makes
a self-referential record (`struct N { struct N *next; }`) terminate for free.
A record whose layout cannot be read completely is not registered at all, and
an unknown tag simply takes the previous element numbering, so both fall back
to the old behavior rather than mis-mapping.

Also lower a braced scalar (`int x = { v };`) to the plain assignment it is,
instead of writing the synthetic `deref` field of a scalar.

The registry is built by a node walk rather than a tree-sitter query because
the record kinds differ per grammar (class_specifier is C++-only and would not
compile against C), so this covers both frontends with no language branch.
